<DocsLayout title="MCP Server for Coding Agents" description="Give Claude Code, Cursor and Codex your design tokens and your team's design decisions so they stop hardcoding values">
  <h1>MCP Server for Coding Agents</h1>

  <p>
    Coding agents hardcode values because they never read your token file. <code>buoy mcp</code>
    gives Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and Windsurf five tools that answer "what tokens does this repo
    have?", "what did the team already decide?" and "did I just drift?" while the agent is writing, so the
    fix happens before the PR. A hook for Claude Code and Cursor checks each edit as it lands.
  </p>

  <h2>Setup</h2>

  <p><strong>Claude Code plugin</strong> (MCP server, edit hook, a <code>design-tokens</code> skill and a <code>/buoy:buoy-check</code> command in one install):</p>

  <pre><code>/plugin marketplace add ahoybuoy/buoy
/plugin install buoy@buoy</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>Cursor plugin</strong>: the same plugin ships a Cursor build with the MCP server, the <code>design-tokens</code>
    skill and rule, a <code>/buoy-check</code> command and a <code>postToolUse</code> hook. It needs Cursor 3.13 or later.
    To install it from a checkout:
  </p>

  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/ahoybuoy/buoy-plugin ~/.cursor/plugins/local/buoy</code></pre>

  <p>Then reload Cursor (Developer: Reload Window). The plugin's source is <a href="https://github.com/ahoybuoy/buoy-plugin">ahoybuoy/buoy-plugin</a>.</p>

  <p><strong>Any MCP client</strong>, or Claude Code without the plugin system:</p>

  <pre><code>npx @buoy-design/cli mcp install claude    # or cursor, codex, windsurf, all</code></pre>

  <p>For Claude Code this writes two files into the repo:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>.mcp.json</code>: registers the <code>buoy</code> MCP server (runs <code>buoy mcp serve</code> over stdio)</li>
    <li><code>.claude/settings.json</code>: a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook on <code>Edit|Write|MultiEdit</code> that runs <code>buoy mcp hook</code>. Pass <code>--no-hook</code> to skip it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Other clients get their own <code>mcp.json</code> (<code>.cursor/</code>, <code>.codex/</code>, <code>.windsurf/</code>) with the
    server only; the Cursor hook comes with the plugin, or you can add it by hand (see below).
    Existing servers and hooks in those files are kept. Commit the files so everyone on the team gets the same agent setup.
  </p>

  <h2>Tools</h2>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What the agent gets</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>context_for_file</code></td><td>Just the design context for the file the agent is about to edit: tokens it and its neighbours already use, the spacing, type and radius steps for the values it contains, components in the same folder or already imported to reuse, drift already in it, and the <a href="#team-decisions">team's decisions</a> for that file. About 500 tokens of context where the full token list of a large system is about 8,000. Meant to be called before each edit; the file may not exist yet.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>list_design_tokens</code></td><td>The repo's tokens (CSS custom properties, Tailwind theme, tokens.json, Sass variables, DESIGN.md). Without arguments, a short summary: counts plus a few per category. With a <code>category</code> or name <code>query</code>, the full list.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>find_token_for_value</code></td><td>The token whose value equals a literal the agent is about to write: <code>#1a73e8</code> → <code>--color-primary</code>. Handles hex shorthand, case and units. An empty result means no token holds that value.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>check_ui_against_design_system</code></td><td>Line-level check of the files just edited: each off-system value with the token to use, plus an exact line edit when the swap is unambiguous. Without files, the full repository analysis.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>design_system_context</code></td><td>A Markdown overview of the system: token categories, components to reuse, and the patterns to avoid. One call at the start of a UI task. <code>detail</code> is <code>minimal</code>, <code>standard</code> (default) or <code>comprehensive</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Repos with no tokens yet still get answers: <code>context_for_file</code>, <code>list_design_tokens</code> and
    <code>find_token_for_value</code> return the values the code already uses most, so the agent reuses those instead of
    inventing new ones. <code>buoy dock tokens</code> turns them into a tokens file.
  </p>

  <h2 id="team-decisions">Team decisions</h2>

  <p>
    <code>context_for_file</code> includes a <code>teamDecisions</code> object, so the agent knows what the team already decided
    before it writes. It doesn't "fix" a value the team kept on purpose, and it doesn't write one the team always wants flagged.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>leaveAlone</code>: values to keep as written. These come from <code>drift.ignore</code> rules in <code>.buoy.yaml</code> that apply to the file, with their reasons, plus values your team declined on pull requests, in this file or kept across several files.</li>
    <li><code>alwaysFlag</code>: <code>drift.enforce</code> and <code>drift.promote</code> rules that apply to the file.</li>
    <li><code>sources</code>: where the decisions came from, or why the pull-request part is missing.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a file covered by a <code>drift.ignore</code> rule, with the CLI logged out:</p>

  <pre><code>"teamDecisions": &#123;
  "leaveAlone": ["8px in src/components/Hero.css: Hero padding matches the marketing grid"],
  "alwaysFlag": [],
  "sources": [
    ".buoy.yaml",
    "PR reviews: not logged in (run `buoy ahoy login` to include decisions from PR reviews)"
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>

  <p>
    The <code>.buoy.yaml</code> rules work everywhere, with no account. The pull-request decisions come from Buoy Cloud and
    need three things: the CLI is logged in (<code>buoy ahoy login</code>), the repo has a GitHub <code>origin</code> remote, and
    that repo is on Buoy in your account. The lookup has a 2-second timeout and is cached for five minutes per file. When it
    can't answer (logged out, offline, or the repo isn't on Buoy), <code>sources</code> says why and the rest of the tool's
    result is unchanged; it never makes the tool fail. When <code>leaveAlone</code> has entries, the result's
    <code>note</code> tells the agent to keep those values even where they look off-scale.
  </p>

  <h2>Hooks</h2>

  <p>
    After the agent writes or edits a <code>.tsx</code>, <code>.jsx</code>, <code>.vue</code>, <code>.svelte</code>,
    <code>.css</code>, <code>.scss</code> or <code>.html</code> file, <code>buoy mcp hook</code> checks it and hands any drift back
    to the agent. Only the lines the edit changed are checked (uncommitted changes against <code>HEAD</code>; a new, untracked file
    is checked whole), so touching one line of an old file doesn't send the agent after every literal already in it. Files in
    <code>node_modules</code>, <code>dist</code> and framework build folders are skipped, and files that are already tokenised
    produce no output. A hook failure never blocks the agent: it exits 0 and logs <code>Buoy hook skipped: …</code> to stderr.
  </p>

  <h3>Claude Code</h3>

  <p>
    The plugin and <code>mcp install claude</code> register <code>buoy mcp hook</code> as a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook. It reads
    Claude Code's payload on stdin and, when the edit drifted, exits 2 with feedback on stderr, which Claude acts on right away:
  </p>

  <pre><code>Buoy: 2 design drift issues in src/components/Hero.css
- src/components/Hero.css:2 Hardcoded color #1a2744 has a token: --color-navy -> use var(--color-navy)
- src/components/Hero.css:3 Hardcoded spacing 8px (no matching token)
Replace the literal values with the tokens above, then continue.</code></pre>

  <p>
    When none of the values has a token, the last line instead says to use the nearest token from
    <code>list_design_tokens</code>, or keep the value if it is deliberate.
  </p>

  <h3>Cursor</h3>

  <p>
    <code>buoy mcp hook --cursor</code> reads Cursor's <code>postToolUse</code> payload and prints the same feedback as
    <code>additional_context</code> JSON, which Cursor attaches to the edit. A clean file prints nothing. It always exits 0,
    because in Cursor exit code 2 blocks the action. The project root comes from the payload's <code>cwd</code>, then its first
    <code>workspace_roots</code> entry, then <code>CURSOR_PROJECT_DIR</code>.
  </p>

  <pre><code>&#123;"additional_context":"Buoy: 2 design drift issues in src/components/Hero.css\n- src/components/Hero.css:2 Hardcoded color #1a2744 has a token: --color-navy -> use var(--color-navy)\n- src/components/Hero.css:3 Hardcoded spacing 8px (no matching token)\nReplace the literal values with the tokens above, then continue."&#125;</code></pre>

  <p>The Cursor plugin (1.4.0 and later) registers it on <code>Write</code>. Without the plugin, add it to <code>.cursor/hooks.json</code>:</p>

  <pre><code>&#123;
  "version": 1,
  "hooks": &#123;
    "postToolUse": [
      &#123; "command": "npx -y @buoy-design/cli@latest mcp hook --cursor", "matcher": "Write", "timeout": 120 &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h3>Other agents</h3>

  <p>
    Any agent runner can call the hook directly: <code>buoy mcp hook --file src/Button.tsx</code> checks that file and exits 2 with
    the feedback on stderr when it drifted.
  </p>

  <h2>Without an install step</h2>

  <p>Buoy is listed in the <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0/servers?search=io.github.ahoybuoy/buoy">official MCP Registry</a> as <code>io.github.ahoybuoy/buoy</code>. Any MCP client can also register the server by hand:</p>

  <pre><code>&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "buoy": &#123; "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@buoy-design/cli", "mcp", "serve"] &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h2>Pairs with the GitHub App</h2>

  <p>
    The MCP server catches drift while the agent writes; the <a href="/docs/integrations/github-actions">GitHub App</a>
    catches what slipped through on the PR, from agents and people alike, and a <a href="/docs/features/ai-guardrails">DESIGN.md</a> gives both the same rules.
    Values your team declines on a PR become <a href="#team-decisions">team decisions</a> the agent sees next time.
    Repos with no tokens yet can run <code>buoy dock tokens</code> to extract a set from the values already in use.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/marketplace/buoy-design"><strong>Install the GitHub App</strong></a>. It is free, and installing it is
    all it takes: the first pull request is reviewed with no Buoy account or sign-up. Sign in to the dashboard later if you want
    history and trends; your reviews come with you.
  </p>

